1
00:00:00,090 --> 00:00:01,150
Hello, everyone.

2
00:00:01,170 --> 00:00:07,230
My name is Olga and in this lesson we are going to speak about SDLC.

3
00:00:07,860 --> 00:00:09,660
What is SDLC?

4
00:00:09,990 --> 00:00:17,040
Well, it's the basis for everybody who works in information technology and everyone who wants to start

5
00:00:17,040 --> 00:00:19,290
a career in information technology.

6
00:00:19,680 --> 00:00:23,610
SDLC is the process of software creation.

7
00:00:24,090 --> 00:00:30,690
SDLC is the abbreviation and it stands for software development lifecycle.

8
00:00:31,440 --> 00:00:37,800
There are different ways to break software development into phases and stages, and you can find a lot

9
00:00:37,800 --> 00:00:39,930
of variations across the Internet.

10
00:00:40,530 --> 00:00:49,200
Try typing in SDLC or software development lifecycle and you will have a lot of results, pictures and

11
00:00:49,200 --> 00:00:50,190
information.

12
00:00:51,270 --> 00:00:55,530
However, let's look at some of the most popular representations together.

13
00:00:56,820 --> 00:01:06,300
In this picture, you can see the process of software creation in a diagram like that, and it starts

14
00:01:06,300 --> 00:01:11,310
with planning and it goes all the way to the deployment stage.

15
00:01:12,900 --> 00:01:18,030
Another picture is an arrow diagram. And you can see that here

16
00:01:18,030 --> 00:01:26,160
there are seven stages and the first one is again planning, then goes analysis, design and so on again

17
00:01:26,790 --> 00:01:29,580
to the final stage, which is maintenance.

18
00:01:33,040 --> 00:01:38,180
The next picture shows another another representation.

19
00:01:38,200 --> 00:01:40,510
It's a very colorful and nice one.

20
00:01:40,690 --> 00:01:43,870
Here the names are again, a little bit different.

21
00:01:43,870 --> 00:01:50,470
And we can see that it starts with ideation and goes to requirements design, development, testing,

22
00:01:50,470 --> 00:01:53,140
deployment and again, maintenance.

23
00:01:53,380 --> 00:02:02,650
So as you can see, all of those representations are a little bit different, but still the core is

24
00:02:02,650 --> 00:02:12,850
the same, the main stages are the same, and the difference is only in how many stages each company

25
00:02:12,850 --> 00:02:15,100
or each representation gives.

26
00:02:15,100 --> 00:02:18,040
And what are the names of those stages?

27
00:02:18,190 --> 00:02:23,230
So for our lesson, I have defined six stages.

28
00:02:23,500 --> 00:02:31,030
They are stage one - requirements collection, stage two - design, stage three - development, stage four -

29
00:02:31,030 --> 00:02:37,510
testing, stage five - deployment and stage six - maintenance.

30
00:02:38,230 --> 00:02:41,290
We can call them stages or phases.

31
00:02:42,010 --> 00:02:49,630
This is not important, but these are the components or the main parts of the software development.

32
00:02:50,230 --> 00:02:53,440
So let's dive in and let's look at the first one.

33
00:02:54,220 --> 00:02:58,330
The first one is phase one requirements collection.

34
00:02:58,570 --> 00:03:07,000
We can also call it requirements gathering, initial analysis, planning, ideation, maybe. What is

35
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:08,500
happening in this stage?

36
00:03:09,670 --> 00:03:10,330
Let's see.

37
00:03:10,840 --> 00:03:14,800
Business and business requirements are gathered and documented.

38
00:03:15,040 --> 00:03:17,770
Major stakeholders give their input.

39
00:03:18,400 --> 00:03:20,530
Project scope is outlined.

40
00:03:20,530 --> 00:03:27,970
Budget resources, deadlines and potential risk and quality assurance requirements are defined.

41
00:03:29,020 --> 00:03:30,820
Who is involved here?

42
00:03:32,140 --> 00:03:36,580
Business analyst, subject matter expert, major stakeholders.

43
00:03:36,580 --> 00:03:44,950
And here we can say that major stakeholders are the customer, partners, other institutions development

44
00:03:44,950 --> 00:03:47,710
team, of course, and and so on.

45
00:03:48,460 --> 00:03:51,040
Project Manager and PMO.

46
00:03:51,040 --> 00:04:01,090
PMO is project management office and it's a person or people who support project managers in day to

47
00:04:01,090 --> 00:04:04,720
day activities, administration, documentation and so on.

48
00:04:06,130 --> 00:04:13,600
All of the words that are involved here like requirements gathered, documented stakeholders and so

49
00:04:13,600 --> 00:04:13,920
on.

50
00:04:13,930 --> 00:04:23,980
This is our vocabulary for each phase and we are going to look at them, explain them. Requirements.

51
00:04:24,100 --> 00:04:30,670
Requirement is a thing that is wanted or needed for this software.

52
00:04:30,940 --> 00:04:32,050
It's a must.

53
00:04:32,050 --> 00:04:34,090
We can say it's a condition.

54
00:04:34,600 --> 00:04:42,970
An example of the requirement can be, for example, the application should not disclose any personal

55
00:04:42,970 --> 00:04:52,600
information of the user. To gather means to collect, to take all the requirements and to put them in

56
00:04:52,600 --> 00:05:01,780
some order, collect them. To document means to create documents on something so that we have information

57
00:05:01,780 --> 00:05:02,980
in written form.

58
00:05:03,790 --> 00:05:10,270
The difference is in how we pronounce the word when we use it as a noun.

59
00:05:10,270 --> 00:05:15,910
For example, I have a document. What it is? It's a dOcument.

60
00:05:15,910 --> 00:05:20,470
We put the stress on the beginning of this word - dOcument.

61
00:05:20,680 --> 00:05:23,320
But the verb, to do what?

62
00:05:23,320 --> 00:05:24,790
To documEnt.

63
00:05:24,970 --> 00:05:28,960
We put the stress here on this syllable.

64
00:05:28,960 --> 00:05:34,750
To documEnt. The next word is a stakeholder.

65
00:05:35,020 --> 00:05:43,990
It's a person or an organization who is actively involved in the project or who can be affected by the

66
00:05:43,990 --> 00:05:44,740
project.

67
00:05:44,980 --> 00:05:53,500
So basically everyone who is somehow connected to our project is a stakeholder.

68
00:05:56,770 --> 00:05:57,460
Let's move on.

69
00:05:57,460 --> 00:05:59,080
We have some more words here.

70
00:06:00,100 --> 00:06:01,150
Input.

71
00:06:01,150 --> 00:06:06,730
Input is ideas or knowledge that someone gives to a project.

72
00:06:07,870 --> 00:06:11,170
We can call it input. Project scope -

73
00:06:11,710 --> 00:06:17,830
all aspects of the project, including all activities, resources, deliverables, etc.

74
00:06:17,950 --> 00:06:25,300
So basically project scope is how big the project is. Oo outline.

75
00:06:25,570 --> 00:06:29,560
To outline means to describe something in in general.

76
00:06:29,560 --> 00:06:33,460
Yeah, in a general way without giving too many details.

77
00:06:33,460 --> 00:06:42,310
So it's like to give a big picture to describe generally. And subject matter expert, SME is a person

78
00:06:42,310 --> 00:06:51,040
who has knowledge and experience in a specific subject in business area or some technical area.

79
00:06:51,070 --> 00:06:55,480
So basically this is an expert in some area.

80
00:06:55,480 --> 00:06:59,020
That's why we call it subject matter expert.

81
00:07:01,060 --> 00:07:01,720
Okay, good.

82
00:07:01,720 --> 00:07:02,670
Let's move on.

83
00:07:02,680 --> 00:07:06,940
The next phase is number two and it's design.

84
00:07:07,240 --> 00:07:13,090
We can also call it prototyping, systems design, architecture design.

85
00:07:13,750 --> 00:07:15,880
What is happening in this phase?

86
00:07:17,320 --> 00:07:21,790
Software development requirements are translated into design.

87
00:07:22,450 --> 00:07:30,490
The entire system and its elements needs to be designed, including high level design and low level

88
00:07:30,490 --> 00:07:31,120
design.

89
00:07:32,500 --> 00:07:40,750
This stage includes the design of user interfaces, system interfaces, network and network requirements,

90
00:07:40,750 --> 00:07:50,500
databases. Operation training and maintenance plans are drawn up so that developers know what they

91
00:07:50,500 --> 00:07:53,470
need to do throughout every stage of the cycle.

92
00:07:54,850 --> 00:07:57,760
And let's have a look at who's involved here.

93
00:07:58,300 --> 00:08:07,330
It's Business Analyst, Solution, Architect, Project Manager, Project Team, which includes developers,

94
00:08:07,630 --> 00:08:19,810
testing specialists, testers, QA specialists, UI and UX designers and whoever else we have on the

95
00:08:19,810 --> 00:08:20,500
project.

96
00:08:21,580 --> 00:08:26,980
Client representatives, business representatives and stakeholders.

97
00:08:29,560 --> 00:08:33,520
Now we have - entire, high-level design, low-level design,

98
00:08:33,520 --> 00:08:36,309
and to draw up. Let's look at those words.

99
00:08:37,419 --> 00:08:45,700
The entire means the whole. High level design, the abbreviation is HLD.

100
00:08:45,730 --> 00:08:48,520
It's the system's architectural design.

101
00:08:48,520 --> 00:08:56,500
It means, again, this big picture of how the software or the application is going to be created,

102
00:08:56,500 --> 00:09:01,780
what components, what modules, it's going to have. A very general picture.

103
00:09:01,990 --> 00:09:04,390
And then low-level design.

104
00:09:04,390 --> 00:09:09,490
LLD is the design of those components and those modules.

105
00:09:09,490 --> 00:09:15,250
So it's a detailed description of all of the components, configurations, processes.

106
00:09:16,630 --> 00:09:23,290
And the last word here is to draw up. It means to prepare a draft of something.

107
00:09:23,770 --> 00:09:28,120
The third stage or phase is development.

108
00:09:29,290 --> 00:09:38,950
It's, we cannot say that it's the most important one, but basically on this stage the actual code is

109
00:09:38,950 --> 00:09:39,940
being created.

110
00:09:39,940 --> 00:09:42,640
So it's a very important stage.

111
00:09:42,640 --> 00:09:45,640
We can also call it software development.

112
00:09:45,820 --> 00:09:51,280
We can also call it implementation, very often or building.

113
00:09:52,720 --> 00:09:56,650
Let's see what is happening at this stage.

114
00:09:58,530 --> 00:10:00,480
Using the design document

115
00:10:00,480 --> 00:10:04,830
software developers write code for all the components.

116
00:10:06,030 --> 00:10:10,710
Program code is built per the design document specifications.

117
00:10:11,730 --> 00:10:15,960
Every developer has to stick to the agreed blueprint.

118
00:10:16,890 --> 00:10:19,620
Developers utilize different tools.

119
00:10:19,620 --> 00:10:24,270
For example, compilers, debuggers and interpreters.

120
00:10:25,260 --> 00:10:31,140
The tasks are divided among the team members according to the area of specialization.

121
00:10:31,740 --> 00:10:39,990
That means front end developers, backend developers, database administrators, etc. It is the most

122
00:10:39,990 --> 00:10:42,000
time consuming phase.

123
00:10:43,170 --> 00:10:47,160
The result of this phase is a working software product.

124
00:10:48,810 --> 00:10:49,930
Who is involved here?

125
00:10:49,950 --> 00:10:58,140
While this is a very technical part of the job, so mostly the developers, designers, business analyst,

126
00:10:58,140 --> 00:11:03,030
project manager and some other stakeholders if needed.

127
00:11:05,670 --> 00:11:07,410
Now let's look at some words.

128
00:11:09,540 --> 00:11:14,340
So a component is a part. The stress falls on

129
00:11:14,340 --> 00:11:16,500
O, yeah, the compOnent.

130
00:11:19,020 --> 00:11:23,190
Per or as per means according to.

131
00:11:23,640 --> 00:11:27,270
We can quickly jump back to this sentence.

132
00:11:27,420 --> 00:11:32,580
Program code is built per the design document specification.

133
00:11:32,580 --> 00:11:35,280
So it means according to that design document.

134
00:11:37,620 --> 00:11:45,810
Specification or technical specification is a document that explains what a product product will do

135
00:11:46,170 --> 00:11:48,720
and how you will achieve those goals.

136
00:11:48,990 --> 00:11:51,510
So it's a roadmap, it's a plan.

137
00:11:51,720 --> 00:12:00,480
Everything from the technical point of view, everything important is written inside that document.

138
00:12:02,490 --> 00:12:12,330
To stick to something means to keep doing a particular thing and not change to anything else or to follow

139
00:12:12,330 --> 00:12:13,740
the specification.

140
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:17,550
In this case, again, we can jump back.

141
00:12:18,030 --> 00:12:22,260
Every developer has to stick to the agreed blueprint.

142
00:12:23,340 --> 00:12:29,790
It means that every developer has to follow very precisely the agreed specification.

143
00:12:30,530 --> 00:12:36,750
Blueprint is a detailed plan of how to do something.

144
00:12:37,350 --> 00:12:45,360
So in our case, specification, blueprint, requirements are kind of like synonyms.

145
00:12:45,930 --> 00:12:49,110
And an interesting word to utilize.

146
00:12:49,590 --> 00:12:51,960
And you have a transcript here.

147
00:12:51,990 --> 00:12:54,420
Utilize means to use.

148
00:12:56,200 --> 00:12:58,000
If we jump back again.

149
00:13:01,960 --> 00:13:03,100
We can see it here.

150
00:13:03,100 --> 00:13:10,780
Developers utilize different tools and we can say developers use different tools.

151
00:13:14,650 --> 00:13:15,700
Compiler.

152
00:13:15,880 --> 00:13:24,160
Compiler is a computer program that translates a set of instructions written in a programming language

153
00:13:24,160 --> 00:13:32,620
such as C, Java, etc. into machine language to execute or run these instructions. In simple words

154
00:13:32,710 --> 00:13:42,580
compiler is just a program that transforms the code in a particular programming language into the

155
00:13:42,580 --> 00:13:47,110
commands for the computer, so that the computer understands what to do.

156
00:13:48,580 --> 00:13:55,120
Debugger is a program that allows you to go through your program code line by line.

157
00:13:55,810 --> 00:14:02,290
This is a very useful, this is very useful when you try to identify incorrect code and analyze how a

158
00:14:02,290 --> 00:14:03,730
program flows.

159
00:14:03,760 --> 00:14:13,600
So a debugger is basically used when you test what you have coded and you want to see how it works and

160
00:14:13,600 --> 00:14:18,490
to find some errors and mistakes if you have any in your code.

161
00:14:19,690 --> 00:14:27,610
Interpreter is another computer program that directly executes instructions written in a programming

162
00:14:27,880 --> 00:14:29,650
or scripting language.

163
00:14:31,450 --> 00:14:32,830
And the general term.

164
00:14:32,830 --> 00:14:37,990
Time consuming means using or taking up a lot of time.

165
00:14:37,990 --> 00:14:44,740
As we said, development phase is a very time-consuming phase.

166
00:14:44,740 --> 00:14:51,280
It's actually the most time-consuming phase because the actual program is being created here.

167
00:14:54,250 --> 00:15:00,790
So at this point, I would like to ask you to jump to the quiz after this part of the lesson and do

168
00:15:00,790 --> 00:15:02,170
the quiz for those words.

169
00:15:02,170 --> 00:15:04,900
And then we will continue with the next part.

